ws up frequently in medical contexts meaning sweating, but there are also biology entries where it refers to leaf venation—related to vascularization but not quite the same thing. تَعْرِيق appears in some entries about nerve arrangement, though the evidence seems incomplete.\n\nMoving to stage 3, تَكَوُّن الأَوعِيَة looks confirmed as a multi-word expression with an idafi structure. It's used more broadly in vascular contexts and older medical literature, covering the formation of vessels more comprehensively than the simpler تَوَعِّي term.\n\nFor تَوَعِّي, this is the more precise technical term in standardized medical Arabic, referring specifically to the physiological process of tissue acquiring vascular characteristics. It's narrower and more contemporary than تَكَوُّن الأَوعِيَة, so I'm updating both lemmas accordingly.\n\nNow I need to assess the current definition. It describes the organic process where body tissues become vascular and develop capillaries, but I'm thinking this needs revision to better capture the nuance of the term.\n\nThe issue is that the current definition is a literal translation from English, and there's a grammatical problem with the verb \"تطور\" — in standard Arabic it's intransitive and shouldn't take a direct object the way it's used here to mean \"develops.\" The phrasing \"تصبح وعائية\" is also less precise than \"تكتسب طابعها الوعائي.\" I'm considering a more accurate terminological definition where the tissue acquires its vascular character. Now I'm extracting the root morphemes for each word in the compound term, pulling the etymological evidence from classical Arabic lexicography to establish the semantic foundation of each component. I'm noting the distinctions between these two terms—تَكَوُّن الأَوعِيَة captures the structural, developmental perspective while تَوَعِّي emphasizes the functional, physiological aspect and is the more precise modern medical term. The concept itself is universal scientifically but lacks a natura